    "summary": "No remaining concrete defects in the two supplied files, so the previous MAJOR and MINOR findings are closed. This is a read-only review of the supplied text only; I did not run tests, and the reported 37 unit tests, lint and typecheck results are not independently verified. The snapshot hash was not independently checked.\n\nClosure of previous findings:\n- Nonboolean `cancelled`: null is handled first as missing_data, then `typeof order.cancelled !== \"boolean\"` returns invalid_data. The undefined, 0, \"false\", {} and [] cases therefore never qualify and are covered by tests.\n- Sparse or malformed intervals: `Array.from(intervals)` turns holes into undefined, which fails the `!interval` check. A non-array input is rejected by `Array.isArray` before any iteration, so no throw path is evident. The test cases `[null]`, `[undefined]`, `[0]`, `new Array(3)`, `{}` and `undefined` all return unavailable.\n\nBehavior checked against the approved rules:\n- Unique coverage: the sort-and-union loop counts each position once, so replays cannot raise coverage.\n- 90 percent threshold: the BigInt cross-multiplication is exact, and the 89999/90000 boundary is tested.\n- Seven-day window: `elapsed` is checked as `>= 0` and `<= 7d` in UTC milliseconds, so both ends are inclusive. Cancelled orders do not qualify.\n- Scope: the module does no identity joining, persistence, denominator or UI work.\n\nObservation, not a defect: intervals with `endMs > durationMs` are rejected rather than clipped. This is documented in the test name and fails closed. If a future caller's player can report end positions slightly past duration, that is a caller-contract or scope question and not a defect in this task.",
